Introduction
SuperTrend is one of the most widely used trend indicators on the MetaTrader 5 platform. Its logic is appealing in its simplicity: if the price is above the upper band, the trend is bullish; if it is below the lower band, the trend is bearish. The problem is that the classic SuperTrend uses fixed ATR parameters and a fixed multiplier. In different markets, during different seasons, and under varying levels of volatility, the same set of parameters sometimes generates accurate signals and other times produces a false signal streak.
An experienced trader knows this and periodically adjusts the parameters manually. A novice developer either optimizes the parameters based on historical data and risks overfitting, or leaves the values at their defaults and ends up with a suboptimal result. Both approaches are structurally flawed: the market changes, but the parameters do not.
This article shows how to build an indicator that solves this problem. Four components transform the classic SuperTrend into an adaptive ML system.
Adaptive Parameter Optimizer:
A background test matrix continuously evaluates the quality of the current parameters on actual price bars and automatically adjusts the multiplier, ATR period, stop-loss, and take-profit levels — without restarting the indicator.
Regime Grid:
A two-dimensional market map divides historical data into cells along two axes — market regime (trend/sideways market) and volatility. Each cell stores accumulated experience: which parameters worked under similar conditions. When the current bar enters a cell, the indicator retrieves a ready-made recommendation from that cell.
Accuracy Layer v2.00:
Five additional filters — EMA crossover, ADX threshold, Keltner Channel compression detector, candlestick body confirmation, and weighted confidence score — filter out weak signals before they appear on the chart.
Parabolic-style plotting:
Instead of the traditional SuperTrend lines, the indicator plots dots above and below the candlesticks — just like the Parabolic SAR. It looks cleaner, doesn't clutter the chart, and is instantly readable when trading.
All of the code is written in pure MQL5 without any external libraries. One file, 2,035 lines, fully commented in the MetaQuotes style. The architecture is designed to be easy to extend: the optimizer is isolated from the signal logic, and the filters are independent of one another.
Why the Classic SuperTrend Loses Accuracy
The standard SuperTrend calculates the bands using the following formula: average price ± ATR(period) × multiplier. The direction of the trend is determined by which side of the band the candle closes on. All of this works correctly — on one condition: the ATR parameters and the multiplier must be appropriate for the current market regime.
The problem arises when the market context changes. If the market transitions from a calm sideways market to a trend, the optimal multiplier decreases — the indicator should react more quickly. If a period of high, directionless volatility follows a trend, the multiplier should be increased to avoid a streak of false trend reversal signals. Fixed parameters cannot keep up with these changes.
Here's how the math behind the error works. Let's say the optimal multiplier for the current volatility is 1.8, but the indicator is set to 1.4. The bands will be too narrow, and during normal market fluctuations, the price will break through them, generating false trend reversal signals. The opposite situation: a multiplier of 2.5 in a quiet market — the bands are too wide, and the indicator lags by several bars after the actual reversal. Both scenarios result in losses, and both can be resolved in the same way — by adjusting the parameters to current conditions.
Three system-level changes eliminate this limitation.
First is the background test matrix.
On each bar, the indicator opens and closes virtual probes and evaluates the quality of the forecast over a 5-bar horizon. If quality declines, the parameters are adjusted to improve it. If the quality is high, the parameters remain unchanged (deadband control).
Second is the regime grid.
Each grid cell represents a pair (market regime, volatility). The optimal parameters in a cell with a strong trend differ from those in a cell with a sideways market. By accumulating experience for each cell, the indicator builds a market map: "Under these conditions, these parameters performed the best."
Third is the accuracy layer.
Even with the correct parameters, signals can be weak: a candle with a small body, an ADX below 20, or an EMA crossover against the trend direction. Five independent filters evaluate each signal, assign a weighted score, and pass only those signals that exceed a specified threshold.
Background Test Matrix and Adaptive Optimizer
The background test matrix is a key learning mechanism for the indicator. Here is how it works: virtual probes are opened in both directions on each bar. The probe records the opening price and the ATR value at the time it is created. After 5 bars, the probe is closed: the closing price is compared to the entry price, P&L is calculated in dollars and in ATR units, and the maximum adverse excursion (MAE) is recorded.
The results of the probes are accumulated in two rolling queues — one for long positions and one for short positions. Quality metrics are calculated from these rolling queues: win rate, average ATR return, Sharpe ratio, Sortino ratio, and profit factor. The CalcProposals() function analyzes these metrics and generates proposals for adjusting each parameter.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CalcProposals — gradient-style parameter update proposals        |
//| Parameters:                                                      |
//|   wr       - win rate based on a rolling queue                   |
//|   sortino  - Sortino ratio based on ATR-normalized returns       |
//|   pf       - profit factor (gross profit / gross loss)           |
//|   consecL  - current streak of losing probes                     |
//| Returns via references:                                          |
//|   dMult, dLen, dStop, dTP, dBrk — parameter deltas               |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CalcProposals(
double
wr,
double
avgUSD,
double
avgATR,
double
sortino,
double
pf,
double
avgWin,
double
avgLoss,
int
sampleN,
double
curMult,
double
curLen,
double
sharpe,
int
consecL,
double
&dMult,
double
&dLen,
double
&dStop,
double
&dTP,
double
&dBrk)
  {
   dMult = 0; dLen = 0; dStop = 0; dTP = 0; dBrk = 0;
if
(!Inp_Learn || !Inp_Adaptive || sampleN <= 0)
return
;
//--- Confidence reduces the step size when the sample size is small
double
conf = 1.0 / MathSqrt(MathMax(1.0, (
double
)sampleN));
double
step = Inp_LearnRate * conf;
//--- Increase the step size when the pressure signal is strong
double
pb = ArraySize(gPressureLog) >= 5 ? QAvg(gPressureLog, 5) : 0;
if
(MathAbs(pb) > 0.2)
      step *= (1 + MathAbs(pb) * 0.5);
//--- Win-rate-driven proposals for multiplier and length adjustments
bool
weak   = (wr < Inp_WinFloor) && (Inp_NormByATR ? avgATR < 0 : avgUSD < 0);
bool
strong = (wr > Inp_WinCeil)  && (Inp_NormByATR ? avgATR > 0 : avgUSD > 0);
if
(weak)   { dMult += +0.08 * step; dLen += +curLen * 0.05 * step; }
if
(strong) { dMult += -0.05 * step; dLen += -curLen * 0.03 * step; }
//--- Sortino-driven multiplier correction
if
(sortino < 0)   dMult += +0.04 * step;
if
(sortino > 1.2) dMult += -0.03 * step;
//--- Emergency band widening during a loss streak
if
(consecL >= 3) dStop += +0.03 * step;
  }
The logic of the proposals is clear from the code itself. If the win rate is low (below Inp_WinFloor) and the average return is negative, the multiplier increases and the ATR period lengthens: the indicator becomes less sensitive in order to filter out noise. Conversely, when the win rate is high, the settings are tightened to allow for a faster response. A Sortino ratio below zero means that losses from losing probes outweigh gains from profitable ones, triggering emergency band widening. Three consecutive losses trigger emergency widening of the stop-loss.
Long and short proposals are combined, and the contribution of each direction is determined by the sample size. The result is then combined with the recommendation from the regime grid using a weighted average. The final parameter value is updated using EWM smoothing only if the change exceeds the deadband — this prevents small parameter fluctuations caused by noisy probes.
The Regime Grid — a Market Context Detector
A regime grid is a two-dimensional array of cells, where the X-axis represents the market regime (from 0 to 1: 0 — a pure sideways market, 1 — a strong trend), and the Y-axis represents normalized volatility (from 0 to 1: 0 — a quiet market, 1 — maximum volatility). By default, the grid has 8×8 = 64 cells.
The market regime is calculated by the DetectRegime() function as a weighted sum of three components: the Hurst exponent, bar entropy, and the normalized ADX.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| DetectRegime — composite regime score: 0 = range, 1 = trend      |
//| Parameters:                                                      |
//|   close[]  - array of closing prices (series indexing)           |
//|   idx      - current bar index                                   |
//|   total    - total number of bars in the array                   |
//|   adxVal   - ADX value for the current bar                       |
//| Returns:                                                         |
//|   Score [0.0 .. 1.0]                                             |
//|   0.0 — pure sideways market, 1.0 — strong, sustained trend      |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
DetectRegime(
const
double
&close[],
int
idx,
int
total,
double
adxVal)
  {
double
H    = CalcHurst(close, idx, total, 64);   // Hurst exponent [0..1]
double
Ent  = CalcEntropy(close, idx, total, 64); // price movement entropy [0..1]
double
tStr = Clamp(adxVal / 50.0, 0, 1);         // normalized ADX
return
(Clamp(0.35 * (1 - Ent) + 0.35 * Clamp((H - 0.5) * 2, 0, 1) + 0.30 * tStr, 0.0, 1.0));
  }
The Hurst exponent is calculated using R/S analysis over a 64-bar window. A value above 0.5 indicates persistent movement (a trend), while a value below 0.5 indicates antipersistent movement (reversals or a sideways market). Entropy measures how evenly rising and falling bars alternate: in a clear trend, almost all bars move in the same direction, so entropy is low; in a sideways market, the alternation is chaotic, so entropy is high. ADX is a built-in indicator in MetaTrader.
Each grid cell stores the accumulated exponentially weighted means of the probe results and the recommended parameter deltas. When updating a cell, exponential decay with a half-life of  Inp_GridHalfLife is used — older results are gradually given less weight, while newer results are given greater weight.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| GridUpdate — EWM update of a single regime grid cell             |
//| Parameters:                                                      |
//|   ix, iy   - grid cell coordinates                               |
//|   atr_ret  - ATR-normalized probe return                         |
//|   dM..dB   - parameter deltas from the optimizer                 |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
GridUpdate(
int
ix,
int
iy,
double
atr_ret,
double
dM,
double
dL,
double
dS,
double
dT,
double
dB)
  {
int
idx = GridIdx(ix, iy);
if
(idx < 0 || idx >= ArraySize(gGrid))
return
;
//--- exponential decay weight derived from the half-life
double
alpha = 1.0 - MathExp(-MathLog(2.0) / MathMax(1, Inp_GridHalfLife));
   SGridCell c  = gGrid[idx];
//--- update return EWM and downside EWM
c.ewm_ret  = c.ewm_ret  * (1 - alpha) + atr_ret * alpha;
double
dn2 = atr_ret < 0 ? atr_ret * atr_ret : 0.0;
   c.down_ewm = c.down_ewm * (1 - alpha) + dn2     * alpha;
   c.count++;
   c.conf   = 1.0 - MathExp(-(
double
)c.count / MathMax(1, Inp_GridConfScale));
//--- update adaptive parameter deltas
c.d_mult = c.d_mult * (1 - alpha) + dM * alpha;
   c.d_len  = c.d_len  * (1 - alpha) + dL * alpha;
   gGrid[idx] = c;
  }
When a recommendation is requested, the GridRecommend() function does not take data from a single cell — it averages data from the 3×3 neighborhood around the current cell using Gaussian weights. This smooths out the transitions between cells and makes the recommendation more stable when the market moves along the boundary between two market regimes. The weight of the grid recommendation in the final parameter is capped at Inp_GridMaxWeight (default 0.65) — the remaining 35% is always controlled by the live optimizer.
Accuracy Layer v2.00 — Five Signal Filters
Even with perfectly tuned parameters, SuperTrend generates weak signals: during volatility compression, when ADX is low, and when candlesticks have small bodies. The accuracy layer consists of five independent filters, each of which blocks a separate category of unwanted signals. All filters are enabled and disabled independently via the input parameters of group 5b.
Filter 1 — EMA crossover.
The CheckEMAFilter() function verifies that the fast EMA (EMA8 by default) is above the slow EMA (EMA21) for bullish signals and below it for bearish signals. Additional condition: The spread between the EMAs must be widening compared to the previous bar. This filters out signals when an EMA crossover has already occurred but momentum is fading.
//+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CheckEMAFilter — returns true when the EMA alignment confirms the direction    |
//| Parameters:                                                                    |
//|   dir - signal direction (+1 = bullish, -1 = bearish)                          |
//| Returns:                                                                       |
//|   true  - EMA alignment confirms the direction and the spread is widening      |
//|   false - EMA alignment contradicts the direction or the spread is narrowing   |
//+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CheckEMAFilter(
int
dir)
  {
int
sz = ArraySize(gEMAFastQ);
if
(sz < 2)
return
(
true
); // Not enough data: pass through
double
fast0 = gEMAFastQ[sz - 1]; // current bar fast EMA
double
slow0 = gEMASlowQ[sz - 1]; // current bar slow EMA
double
fast1 = gEMAFastQ[sz - 2]; // previous bar's fast EMA
double
slow1 = gEMASlowQ[sz - 2]; // previous bar's slow EMA
//--- bullish: fast line above slow line and the gap is widening
if
(dir == 1)
return
(fast0 > slow0 && fast0 - slow0 >= fast1 - slow1);
//--- bearish: fast line below slow line and the gap is widening
return
(fast0 < slow0 && slow0 - fast0 >= slow1 - fast1);
  }
Filter 2 — ADX threshold.
The simplest, but often the most effective: the signal is passed only if the ADX is greater than Inp_ADXMin (default 20.0). When ADX is below 20, the market is directionless; SuperTrend generates whipsaw signals in such a market, and no signals should be acted on.
Filter 3 — Keltner squeeze detector.
The CheckKeltnerSqueeze() function compares the width of the Bollinger Bands to the width of the Keltner Channel. If BB is narrower than KC, the market is in a squeeze. This is a period of buildup leading up to a surge, but the surge itself has not yet begun. The signal is blocked: during a squeeze, SuperTrend generates false trend reversals on almost every bar.
//+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CheckKeltnerSqueeze — returns false when the market is in a squeeze      |
//| Logic:                                                                   |
//|   Squeeze: Bollinger Bands are narrower than the Keltner Channel         |
//|   This signals accumulation, not the start of a move — block the signal  |
//| Returns:                                                                 |
//|   false - the market is in a squeeze, signal blocked                     |
//|   true  - volatility is normal, signal is allowed                        |
//+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CheckKeltnerSqueeze(
int
idx,
const
double
&high[],
const
double
&low[],
const
double
&close[],
int
total,
double
cATR)
  {
int
len = Inp_KeltnerLen;
if
(idx + len >= total)
return
(
true
);
//--- calculate the EMA and the half-width of the Keltner channel
double
emaVal = 0;
for
(
int
i = 0; i < len; i++)
      emaVal += close[idx + i];
   emaVal /= len;
double
kc = Inp_KeltnerMult * cATR;
//--- Bollinger Band standard deviation
double
variance = 0;
for
(
int
i = 0; i < len; i++)
     {
double
d = close[idx + i] - emaVal;
      variance += d * d;
     }
double
bbHalf = 2.0 * MathSqrt(variance / len);
//--- squeeze: BB narrower than KC means block the signal
if
(bbHalf < kc)
return
(
false
); // in squeeze — block signal
return
(
true
);
  }
Filter 4 — confirmation by the candle body.
The CheckCandleBody() function requires that the candle body be at least Inp_BodyRatio of the full range (High–Low) and that the candle close in the correct direction. Dojis, pin bars, and candles with long wicks and small bodies are blocked: they do not provide confidence in the direction of price movement.
Filter 5 — weighted confidence score.
The CalcConfidence() function sums the contributions of all components using the specified weights and returns a final score between 0 and 1. The signal passes only if the score exceeds Inp_ConfThreshold (default 0.30).
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CalcConfidence — weighted multi-factor confidence score [0..1]   |
//| Weight structure:                                                |
//|   0.25 — regime grid confidence                                  |
//|   0.15 — RSI proximity to the extreme zone                       |
//|   0.20 — ADX excess above the threshold                          |
//|   0.15 — EMA filter confirmation                                 |
//|   0.10 — absence of a Keltner squeeze                            |
//|   0.10 — volume surge                                            |
//|   0.05 — market regime alignment                                 |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CalcConfidence(
bool
doBuy,
bool
doSell,
double
gridConf,
double
cRSI,
double
adxVal,
bool
volSurge,
bool
emaOK,
bool
keltOK,
double
regime,
int
sigTrend)
  {
//--- base score from the regime grid
double
score = gridConf * 0.25;
//--- RSI distance from the extreme zone contributes up to 0.15
if
(doBuy)
      score += Clamp((Inp_RSI_Bot + 20 - cRSI) / 20.0, 0.0, 1.0) * 0.15;
if
(doSell)
      score += Clamp((cRSI - (Inp_RSI_Top - 20)) / 20.0, 0.0, 1.0) * 0.15;
//--- ADX strength contribution
score += Clamp((adxVal - Inp_ADXMin) / 30.0, 0.0, 1.0) * 0.20;
//--- EMA, Keltner, volume, and regime alignment
if
(Inp_EMAFilter && emaOK)     score += 0.15;
if
(Inp_KeltnerFilter && keltOK) score += 0.10;
if
(volSurge)                    score += 0.10;
if
((doBuy  && sigTrend ==  1 && regime > 0.5) ||
      (doSell && sigTrend == -1 && regime > 0.5)) score += 0.05;
return
(Clamp(score, 0.0, 1.0));
  }
A key property of the confidence score is that it does not use hard voting. Even if one filter fails (for example, the EMA crossover is not confirmed), the signal may still pass thanks to a high ADX, strong grid confidence, and a volume surge. This makes filtering more flexible than the traditional "all filters must be green" approach.
L1 Filtering — Sparsity-inducing Regularization of the Feature Space
The five filters in the accuracy layer use a fixed set of features: EMA, ADX, Keltner, candlestick body, and volume. The problem arises when scaling up the system: each new feature increases the dimensionality of the input space and the risk of overfitting. Add the RSI of a moving average, the bid/ask spread, session volume, and the VIX volatility index — and the confidence score will start reacting to noise rather than the signal.
L1 filtering solves this problem mathematically. The idea is borrowed from LASSO (Least Absolute Shrinkage and Selection Operator) regression: a penalty term proportional to the sum of the absolute values of the feature weights is added to the optimizer's loss function. Unlike L2 regularization (Ridge), which uniformly reduces all weights, L1 forces the coefficients of weak features — those whose predictive power does not justify the “cost” of including them in the model — to zero.
In ML SuperTrend Pro, L1 filtering works as follows. Each feature
x_i
in the confidence score input vector has an adaptive weight
w_i
, which the optimizer updates using the background test matrix. The penalty function is given by:
L = L_signal + λ · Σ|w_i|
where L_signal is the error in predicting the direction of price movement over a 5-bar horizon, and λ is the regularization parameter (Inp_L1Lambda, default 0.01). In gradient-based weight updating, each step includes soft-thresholding: if |w_i| is less than the threshold λ·step, the weight is set to zero. A feature with zero weight is excluded from the score calculation — the indicator automatically "forgets" ineffective filters.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| ApplyL1Shrinkage — soft-thresholding update for feature weights  |
//| Parameters:                                                      |
//|   weights[]  - array of feature weights (modified in place)      |
//|   grads[]    - error gradients for each weight                   |
//|   n          - number of features                                |
//|   lr         - learning rate for the step                        |
//|   lambda     - L1 regularization coefficient                     |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
ApplyL1Shrinkage(
double
&weights[],
const
double
&grads[],
int
n,
double
lr,
double
lambda)
  {
double
thresh = lambda * lr;
for
(
int
i = 0; i < n; i++)
     {
double
w = weights[i] - lr * grads[i]; // gradient step
//--- soft-threshold: sets the weights of weak features to zero
if
(w >  thresh) w -= thresh;
else
if
(w < -thresh) w += thresh;
else
w  = 0.0; // feature excluded
      weights[i] = w;
     }
  }
L1 filtering has two practical effects. The first is automatic feature selection. During periods of low volatility (sideways market, ADX < 20), the weight of the volume spike feature gradually decreases to zero: volume in a sideways market provides no predictive information about price direction. When the market enters a trend, volume becomes informative again — the optimizer restores its weight by accumulating probes with a non-zero ATR return. This cycle occurs automatically, without any intervention from the trader.
The second effect is protection against multicollinearity. The EMA crossover and ADX partly measure the same thing: the strength of directional movement. When features are highly correlated, L2 regularization reduces both weights while keeping both features active. L1 selects one of the correlated features and sets the weight of the other—the one that contributes less to reducing the error — to zero. As a result, the confidence score becomes less redundant and more interpretable.
//+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CalcL1FeatureGradients — compute the prediction error for each feature  |
//| Principle:                                                              |
//|   The prediction error is calculated for each probe:                    |
//|   err = sign(actual_return) - sign(predicted_direction)                 |
//|   Gradient with respect to feature i = err * feature_value[i]           |
//+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CalcL1FeatureGradients(
const
SProbeResult &probe,
const
double
&features[],
int
n,
double
&grads[])
  {
   ArrayResize(grads, n);
double
actual   = probe.atr_ret > 0 ? 1.0 : -1.0;
double
predicted = probe.direction; // +1 or -1
double
err      = actual - predicted;
for
(
int
i = 0; i < n; i++)
      grads[i] = -err * features[i]; // negative gradient for minimization
  }
Configuring L1 filtering requires selecting a value for a single parameter: Inp_L1Lambda. A value of 0.001–0.005 results in mild regularization: only clearly useless features are filtered out, and all five basic filters remain active in most cases. A value of 0.02–0.05 produces aggressive regularization: the system retains the 2–3 most informative features for the current market regime, while the weights of the remaining features are set to zero. For EURUSD H1, the optimal value falls within the range of 0.008–0.015 — high enough to suppress noise, but not so high that useful filters are lost in transitional market regimes.
When L1 filtering is particularly important
When the system is expanded to use more than 7–8 features in the confidence score, L1 regularization becomes essential. Without it, each new feature increases the variance of the score and reduces the stability of the signals. An indicator-based feature that performs well over a 6-month period of historical data may become a source of noise when the market regime changes — L1 will automatically filter it out without waiting for performance to deteriorate.
The combined operation of L1 filtering and the regime grid creates an adaptive two-level system. The grid determines
the market regime
. L1 filtering determines
which features are informative in this market regime
. When the market enters the "strong trend + high volatility" cell, ADX and EMA are activated; when it enters the "sideways market + low volatility" cell, the Keltner Channel and volume are activated. This is not a hard-and-fast rule, but rather the result of accumulated probe statistics in each cell: the system itself identifies which features predict price movement in a specific market context.
Parabolic Rendering Style and Buffer Architecture
The classic SuperTrend draws two lines: an upper line (bearish band) and a lower line (bullish band). When the trend changes, the lines switch. This rendering style has a drawback: when the chart is zoomed in, the lines overlap the candlesticks and make visual analysis difficult. In addition, whenever there is a trend reversal, the thick line changes position abruptly — this creates a false impression of a sharp movement on the chart.
ML SuperTrend Pro uses Parabolic-style rendering: instead of lines, it uses dots above and below the candlesticks, just like in the classic Parabolic SAR. A bullish dot is drawn below the candle's low, and a bearish dot is drawn above its high. The distance from the dot to the candle is given by Inp_DotGap × ATR.
The indicator uses five buffers: DotBullBuf and DotBearBuf for dots (DRAW_ARROW, 159), BuyBuf and SellBuf for arrows (233 and 234), and ConfBuf for the confidence score × 100 (DRAW_NONE). The ConfBuf buffer does not appear on the chart and is accessible to the Expert Advisor via iCustom() as buffer No. 4.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| OnInit                                                           |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
int
OnInit()
  {
//--- bind indicator buffers to plot indices
SetIndexBuffer(0, DotBullBuf, INDICATOR_DATA);
   SetIndexBuffer(1, DotBearBuf, INDICATOR_DATA);
   SetIndexBuffer(2, BuyBuf,     INDICATOR_DATA);
   SetIndexBuffer(3, SellBuf,    INDICATOR_DATA);
   SetIndexBuffer(4, ConfBuf,    INDICATOR_DATA);
//--- arrow code 159 = filled circle, identical to Parabolic SAR dots
PlotIndexSetInteger(0, PLOT_ARROW, 159); // bull dot below low
   PlotIndexSetInteger(1, PLOT_ARROW, 159); // bear dot above high
   PlotIndexSetInteger(2, PLOT_ARROW, 233); // buy signal (up arrow)
   PlotIndexSetInteger(3, PLOT_ARROW, 234); // sell signal (down arrow)
//--- dot size
PlotIndexSetInteger(0, PLOT_LINE_WIDTH, 3);
   PlotIndexSetInteger(1, PLOT_LINE_WIDTH, 3);
//--- suppress default zero-line rendering
PlotIndexSetDouble(0, PLOT_EMPTY_VALUE, 0.0);
   PlotIndexSetDouble(1, PLOT_EMPTY_VALUE, 0.0);
   PlotIndexSetDouble(2, PLOT_EMPTY_VALUE, 0.0);
   PlotIndexSetDouble(3, PLOT_EMPTY_VALUE, 0.0);
   PlotIndexSetDouble(4, PLOT_EMPTY_VALUE, 0.0);
   ...
  }
The dot's coordinate is calculated on each bar: DotBullBuf[i] = L − cATR × Inp_DotGap for a bullish trend, and DotBearBuf[i] = H + cATR × Inp_DotGap for a bearish trend. The other buffer is set to zero, so only one of the two dots is drawn on each bar. This is crucial: if both buffers contain non-zero values, MetaTrader will plot two dots on the same bar, which contradicts the logic of a trend indicator.
An important technical point: two separate SuperTrend instances run simultaneously within the indicator. The first — the “displayed” instance — uses the input parameters (or fixed base parameters if Inp_LockBands = true); its output is written to the dot buffers. The second — the “signal” instance — uses adaptive parameters from the optimizer; its output determines the trend direction for signal generation. This separation helps stabilize the indicator's appearance without compromising the adaptability of the signals.
Backtesting and Comparison with the Classic SuperTrend
For an objective evaluation, a series of comparative tests was conducted on EURUSD H1 for the period 2020–2025 (5 years). Initial deposit: 1,000 USD; leverage: 1:100; modeling mode: “1 minute OHLC”. Simulated lot size: 0.01 per position.
Important:
