A few practical observations on configuration.
Inp_Dial parameter.
A value of 1–5 indicates a slow, conservative optimizer that adjusts gradually and works well with stable instruments. A value of 15–20 indicates a fast optimizer that reacts to market changes within 3–5 bars and is suitable for highly volatile instruments and news periods. To start, it is recommended to leave the value at 10 and observe its behavior.
The Inp_ConfThreshold parameter.
Increasing this value to 0.40–0.45 sharply reduces the signal frequency but retains only high-quality signals. If the goal is to use the indicator for manual trading by analyzing each signal, a value of 0.40 is optimal. For Expert Advisors with low risk per trade, you can leave the value at 0.30.
Inp_L1Lambda parameter.
A value of 0.001–0.005 provides mild regularization; all filters remain active in most market regimes. A value of 0.020–0.050 indicates strict selection: at any given moment, the 2–3 most informative features are active. It is recommended to start with 0.010 and monitor the results: if the win rate remains consistently above 55%, increasing lambda to 0.015 will further reduce the frequency of false signals.
Choosing a time frame.
H1 is the best option. On M15 and lower time frames, the market regime detector (Hurst + Entropy) uses a window that is too short, producing unreliable results. On H4, signals are scarce, and EMA8/21 reacts too slowly on this longer time frame. On D1, the optimizer does not collect enough probes for statistically significant adaptation.
