Common Mistakes When Working with ML Indicators
Practical experience with adaptive systems reveals a recurring set of mistakes that negate the advantages of the ML approach.
Mistake 1
: "I optimize the initial parameters instead of letting the optimizer do its job." The MetaTrader Strategy Tester iterates through parameter values within specified ranges using historical data. For an ML indicator, this is pointless: the optimizer will select the parameters on its own in real time. It only makes sense to optimize Inp_Dial and Inp_ConfThreshold using the tester — these parameters determine the aggressiveness of the adaptation and the filtering threshold, not the SuperTrend parameters themselves.
Mistake 2
: "I'm turning off all the filters — there are too many of them." Each filter eliminates its own class of false signals. Disabling the ADX filter results in signals during a sideways market. Disabling the Keltner filter results in signals during volatility compression before a reversal. Disabling the EMA filter results in signals against the current trend. If the signal frequency seems too low, the correct solution is to lower Inp_ConfThreshold, not to disable the filters.
Mistake 3
: "The indicator worked for a month, then stopped." This is normal behavior for any adaptive system during a sudden change in market regime. The optimizer has adapted to a quiet trend, but the market has shifted to a highly volatile sideways market — it needs 20–30 bars to readjust. At this point, it is best to reduce the position size or switch to a wait-and-see approach.
Mistake 4
: "The confidence score is 100% — I will use the maximum lot size." A score of 100% means that all filters agree, not that the signal is guaranteed to be profitable. The market always involves inherent uncertainty. A high score increases the likelihood of success but does not guarantee it. The position size should be determined by the Expert Advisor's risk manager, not by the score.
Mistake 5
: "Applying it to multiple symbols simultaneously without accounting for correlations." If ML SuperTrend Pro generates a BUY signal for EURUSD and a BUY signal for GBPUSD at the same time, these are not two independent signals. Both currency pairs are correlated via the USD. The actual risk doubles. The wrapper Expert Advisor must take into account the total exposure to the USD and limit it.
Mistake 6
: "Setting Inp_L1Lambda too high from the start." When lambda is above 0.03, L1 regularization may set all feature weights to zero during the first 50–100 bars — the optimizer has not yet accumulated enough probes to distinguish the contributions of individual features. It is recommended to start with lambda = 0.005–0.010, allow the system 100 bars to collect statistics, and then increase lambda to the target value if necessary.
Extending the System
ML SuperTrend Pro v2.00 is a fully developed tool, but there is still room for further development. Several avenues for expansion logically follow from the current architecture.
Multi-timeframe mode.
The indicator currently works on a single timeframe. A logical extension would be to add an HTF filter: the trend direction on the D1 timeframe should match the signal direction on the H1 timeframe. This filters out about 30% of countertrend signals without changing the logic of the main engine.
A neural network instead of a linear optimizer.
The current CalcProposals() is a set of linear rules: if the win rate is low, we increase the multiplier. It can be replaced with a small neural network (2–3 hidden neurons) trained on the accumulated data from the regime grid. In MQL5, such a network can be implemented using a few lines of matrix operations. This will improve adaptation to unusual market conditions that linear rules do not cover.
Adaptive DotGap.
Currently, the distance from the dot to the candle is fixed in ATR units. It makes more sense to scale it based on the volatility within the bar: for candles with long wicks, increase the DotGap so that the dots do not overlap the wicks. This will improve visual readability during news-driven spikes.
Export data to CSV.
Each signal can be saved to a file: date/time, direction, confidence score, values for all five filters, and the result after 5 and 20 bars. This will allow us to perform statistical analysis in Python: plot an ROC curve based on the confidence threshold, find the optimal threshold for a specific instrument, and identify how accuracy varies depending on the time of day and day of the week.
Integration with an Expert Advisor via ConfBuf.
Buffer 4 contains confidence × 100. The Expert Advisor can use it to filter signals and to manage position size: the higher the score, the larger the lot size. This puts into practice the idea of "staking more on higher-confidence signals" without changing the indicator's underlying logic.
Extending L1 filtering to new features.
The current implementation covers five basic features. The architecture of ApplyL1Shrinkage() is not tied to their number — the weights[] array can be expanded to 10–20 elements without changing the shrinkage logic. This paves the way for adding session volume, the bid/ask spread, a VIX proxy (realized volatility over 21 bars), and features based on price action patterns — L1 will automatically select those that are informative for the current market regime.
What the Developer Gets
In this article, we systematically examined why SuperTrend loses its effectiveness when the market regime shifts, and demonstrated how to replace its fixed parameters with a combination of online adaptation, regime memory, and multi-factor signal filtering. All components are integrated into a unified architecture suitable for practical use and automation.
After reading this, you will have more than just a set of ideas — you will have a complete integration specification. The indicator automatically adjusts the ATR period, multiplier, and trading levels based on the results of background probes, takes market context into account through a regime grid, filters out weak signals using several independent mechanisms, and calculates the final signal confidence in a separate buffer.
In practice, this means the system can be connected directly to an Expert Advisor via iCustom(): use Buy (buffer 2), Sell (buffer 3), and Confidence (buffer 4 × 100), set your own confidence threshold, and reproduce the results using a wrapper Expert Advisor in the Strategy Tester. This solves the original problem of SuperTrend's fixed parameters: instead of manual tuning and reoptimization, you get a reproducible, extensible framework for adaptive trading signals, written entirely in MQL5 and ready for integration.
Practical steps to get started:
Compile ML_SuperTrend_GalymYeshanov.mq5 in MetaEditor;
Attach the indicator to the chart and let the system accumulate probe statistics;
Read Buy, Sell, and Confidence using iCustom();
Use a confidence threshold to filter entries;
Verify the reproducibility of the results using the Strategy Tester and a wrapper Expert Advisor.
Usage recommendation: start with the default settings, test on a demo account for at least two months, then deploy an Expert Advisor with risk management. Account for correlations between symbols, and do not treat confidence as a guarantee of results—it is an instrument to support decision-making, not a substitute for risk management.
